sic  a basic circulator and just use regular  ziplock bags something that works really  really well is what's called the water  displacement method essentially you just  put your meat down in the bag we try to  squeeze as much of the air out as you  can you don't have to be super  particular about it and just leave the  top cracked a little bit and then  submerge the bag down in water but not  up to the point where the crack is and  then the pressure from the water will  force essentially all of the air out of  the bag and then you know when only the  tiny little slit is still above the  water go ahead and close it up you get a  seal you get you get air removal that's  essentially on par with what you'd get  from a vacuum bag sealer but it's way  way cheaper in that you don't have to  buy anything except Ziploc bags so I'd  recommend that I haven't priced a  circulator in a while I've been on the  sous-vide game for a long time I got my  sous-vide circulator it's what I asked  for for Christmas in 2014 and so I want  to say I was planning on buying one  until Santa / my dad got it for me but I  think at the time they were maybe like a  hundred bucks give or take so fairly  affordable I think there's still  somewhere in that price range so you  know it's gonna be a significant  purchase but it's probably not gonna  break the bank for most people and  you'll get a lot of use out of it other  things you can do with sous-vide  is one if you really like very  collagenous meats and you need to break  that collagen down but you know you  don't have time to tend to smoker for 12  hours to make sure you know it goes low  and slow well enough or maybe you don't  particularly like braising in the oven  and like how much fat can get drawn out  of the meat or in the braising process  sous-vide can make just just beautiful  say beef ribs beef ribs are absolutely  delicious if they're cooked well if you  have time to break down all of that  collagen it's it's just so good but it's  also kind of difficult to do outside of  like braising or a slow cooker you can  cook say beef ribs sous-vide super super  easily just you know leave him in like  160-170 for 24 to 48 hours it's gonna  break down all of that collagen they're  gonna be fall apart tender super easy to  do way easier to do than pretty much any  other method I would say another benefit  of sous-vide is like I said I'm a big  fan of briny meat you can kind of get a  pseudo brine going on with sous-vide if  you seasoned the meat before you put it  in the bag so you know salted  appropriately then as it cooks some of  the the liquid will get drawn out of the  meat it's gonna combine with the salt  create a brine get absorbed back into  the meat so you don't just have salt on  the outside with me the outside of the  meat you're cooking it's going to  penetrate it fully so it gives you good  flavor through the entire cut of meat  you know that's not super crucial if  you're cooking something fairly thin but  if you're cooking say like a really  thick steak or even chicken breast  making sure that you have that seasoning  dispersed through the entire the entire  hunk of meat is pretty good and clutch  let's see one Oh another thing you can  do is sous-vide which is actually pretty  neat is you can cook eggs perfectly and  so for example if you really like  hard-boiled eggs but you have a tendency  to accidentally overcook them so the  yolk starts turning green and kind of  icky  I don't remember the exact settings you  can google it it's easy enough but you  can set your your circulator at the  perfect temperature that you can just  walk away 30 minutes you come back and  because the temperature control is so  precise you will have absolutely perfect  flawless hard-boiled eggs if you like  soft-boiled eggs and you like the yolk  to be at a very particular type of  consistency maybe you won't fully set  whites and completely runny yolks maybe  you kind of want like a custard a yolk  and doing it in a pot on the stove you  know the difference between having it in  water for five minutes and five minutes  and thirty seconds is enough to ruin the  egg from your perspective because you  don't get the yolk texture you want  never a problem with sous-vide you can  set it at an exact temperature to get  the exact yolk consistency you want set  it and forget it  perfect every time one one problem that  some people will have when they first  play around with sous-vide is you can  set it and forget it to a point and it's  a fairly forgiving cooking technique but  you can absolutely leave things cooking  for too long so for example if you're  trying to make chicken breasts and you  set the water at a hundred and  sixty-five degrees and you walk away for  an hour you're gonna come back it's  gonna be good chicken breast if you set  it for two hours come back it's gonna be  good chicken breast three hours yeah  probably still be fine if you start  cooking it say at the beginning of the  day you leave for work come back eight  hours later it will essentially just  turn the chicken breast to mush so you  can you can overcook things just because  you're leaving something heated for so  long so much of the proteins gonna break  down and in like I said it's going is  it's it's gonna stop having a nice  Pleasant bite as meat should and just  kind of turn mushy and gross but it is  it is still pretty forgiving but don't  just assume that hey I can you know put  something in tonight and tomorrow  morning it's gonna be great for some  things that's the case I give it  something with a fair amount of  connective tissue like ribs you know a -  up to 48 hours of cooking can be  perfectly awesome  whereas like if you want to cook steak  but you you know you still want a nice  bite to it you want to necessarily want  to do that oh that's the other like  super clutch thing about sous-vide if  you want to impress people and you want  to make like a perfect rare steak that's  heated all the way through but you know  not like there's not too much of a gray  ring around the outside or medium-rare  or whatever people like you can do that  so easy with sous-vide so you know if  you like your steak at a hundred and  twenty eight degrees what you can do is  just set the temperature at 125 drop the  steaks in come back hour and a half two  hours later it's gonna be 125 the whole  way through no part of the steak is  gonna be more than 125 no parts gonna be  less all you gotta do is Pat it dry get  a cast-iron pan as hot as it can  possibly get sear for like 30 seconds  aside get a nice crust on it that'll  bring the internal temp up that extra  like 2 3 degrees to get it up to the 128  you want and it's gonna be perfect every  time  super repeatable so anyway it's it's a  fairly flexible kitchen gadget it's not  super cheap but you will be able to get  a lot of use out of it and like I said  for I mean for people who are just into  cooking good food there's a lot of cool  [ __ ] you can do with it  oh another one I'm just pulling stuff  out of my ass right now another really  cool thing you can do with sous vide  going back to eggs is if you're someone  who's concerned about Salmonella from  like cookie dough but you like cookie  dough if you want to make pasteurized  cookie dough at home you can do it with  sous-vide because there is a very  particular temperature range where you  can get the eggs up too which is going  to pasteurize them without actually  cooking anything like it's not gonna set  the white it's not gonna start cooking  the yolk and so you can pasteurize your  eggs using sous-vide I think like a  hundred and I think it's like a hundred  and thirty four degrees Fahrenheit is  the sweet spot by the way every  temperature I've given up to this point  is Fahrenheit if people are listening to  this thinking Celsius and like oh [ __ ]  he's way over cooking a steak it's all  Fahrenheit  yeah I think like 134 Fahrenheit I think  is the magic range get the eggs there  for like 20-30 minutes give or take it  should pasteurize them and then you can  make cookie dough with eggs that behave  as if they're raw eggs but there's no  bacteria it's great same the same thing  with the flour you're not gonna  sous-vide that you're just gonna pop it  in the oven at like 350 for 20-30  minutes that'll heat it up enough to  pasteurize the flour yeah you can use  sous-vide to make beautiful pasteurized  eggs that will behave very very  similarly to raw eggs but be safe to eat  raw so yeah very very versatile like I  said the biggest benefit for people who  are into fitness is very lean typically  not super flavorful cuts of meat you can  cook them perfectly so that they're  still super juicy not overcooked not dry  and stringy very very useful kitchen  tool that I would strongly recommend to  you know anyone who who maybe wants to  spice up their food prep a little bit  yeah so there you have it if you want to  impress your Valentine's Day date and  give them just the perfect meal to  celebrate the occasion hurry up because  you have a lot of work to do in the next  day if you're listening to this the  moment it comes out all right well that  does it for this episode as always thank  you for listening and we'll have another  one in two weeks thanks for listening to  the stronger by science podcast now Greg  and I are not experts in medicine or  health or really anything else for that  matter so before you make any changes to  your diet and exercise habits make sure  you check with a doctor or another  healthcare professional if you enjoyed  this podcast and you'd like to support  it visit stronger by science com2 check  out the products and services that we  offer thanks for listening and we'll see  you next time  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

